\section{Date arithmetic produces year-50,888 timestamps}{

\strong{Symptom:} \code{mutate(ts = as.POSIXct(date, origin = "1970-01-01"))}
produces timestamps in the year 50,888.

\strong{Cause:} The \code{date} column is bigint Unix epoch in \emph{milliseconds}, not
seconds. R's \code{as.POSIXct()} expects seconds.

\strong{Fix:} Use \code{\link[=clean_dates]{clean_dates()}} or divide by 1000 manually:
\code{mutate(ts = clean_dates(date))}.
}

\section{Join produces zero matched rows between chat and streams}{

\strong{Symptom:} \code{inner_join(chat, streams, by = "channel")} returns zero
rows even though both tables have data for the same streamers.

\strong{Cause:} \code{chat$channel} is prefixed with \verb{#} (IRC convention);
\code{streams$channel} is not.

\strong{Fix:} In the shipped \link{twitch_chat_sample}, the prefix has been
stripped, so the join works as-is. If you are pulling raw data from the
source dump, normalize first:
\code{mutate(channel = sub("^#", "", channel))}.
}

\section{\code{setup()} reports Quarto missing but I have Quarto installed}{

\strong{Symptom:} \code{v2v::setup()} shows a red x on Quarto CLI even though you
can run Quarto from the terminal.

\strong{Cause:} R was already open when Quarto was installed. R reads the
\code{PATH} once, when it starts, so a session opened before the install
cannot see Quarto. (Before v2v 0.3.1, \code{setup()} also misreported the CLI
as missing whenever the \code{quarto} R package was absent; that is fixed.)

\strong{Fix:} Quit R, open it again, and run \code{setup()} once more. If the red x
stays, reinstall Quarto from \url{https://quarto.org/docs/get-started/}. The
\code{quarto} R package is a separate, optional wrapper: installing it does not
install the CLI.
}

\section{Cohen's kappa is much lower than I expected}{

\strong{Symptom:} Two coders who seem to agree most of the time still
produce a kappa near 0.20.

\strong{Cause:} Kappa corrects for chance agreement. If one code is rare,
two coders can agree 90\% of the time on the \emph{common} code (negative)
and still produce a low kappa because the agreement on the \emph{rare} code
(positive) is the load-bearing signal.

\strong{Fix:} Two paths: (1) revise the codebook so the rare code is better
defined and shows up more often, or (2) report Krippendorff's alpha
(\code{method = "alpha"}) which handles imbalance more gracefully.
}

\section{\code{data(twitch_chat_sample)} returns NULL or "not found"}{

\strong{Symptom:} \code{data(twitch_chat_sample)} throws "data set
'twitch_chat_sample' not found".

\strong{Cause:} The package is not installed, or you are calling \code{data()}
without \code{library(v2v)} first.

\strong{Fix:} \code{library(v2v)} and try again. If still failing, reinstall from
GitHub (v2v is not on CRAN, so \code{install.packages("v2v")} cannot find it):
\code{remotes::install_github("AURA-Lab-SIUE/v2v-r", upgrade = "never", force = TRUE)}.
}

\section{VS Code says R is not attached, or the connection to the server closed}{

\strong{Symptom:} The status bar in VS Code reads \verb{R: (not attached)} and the
Workspace panel stays empty, or VS Code says \verb{Connection to server got closed. Server will not be restarted} or \verb{read ECONNRESET}. Your code still
runs.

\strong{Cause:} Two helpers run beside R in VS Code: the R extension's session
watcher, which fills the Workspace panel, and the \code{languageserver} package,
which powers code completion. The R extension before version 3.0.0 cannot
connect to R 4.6 or later; a \code{languageserver} that failed to install stops
the second helper.

\strong{Fix:} With the AURA Lab R Companion, run \strong{AURA: Explain an R error} on
the message and select \strong{Update the R extension}. Without it: update R by
REditorSupport in the Extensions view to 3.0.0 or newer, quit VS Code, run
\code{install.packages("languageserver")} in R on its own (answer No if asked to
compile from source), then open VS Code again and allow it to install
\code{sess} if it asks.
}

\section{Stray \verb{[200~} and \verb{[201~} characters around code you run}{

\strong{Symptom:} Code sent from a script to the R terminal arrives wrapped in
\verb{[200~} and \verb{[201~}, and R waits with a \code{+} prompt.

\strong{Cause:} The workspace setting \code{r.bracketedPaste} is on. It sends code in a
format meant for a different R console. Copies of the course workspace made
before September 2026 still carry it.

\strong{Fix:} With the R Companion, run \strong{AURA: Explain an R error} on the
message and select \strong{Turn off bracketed paste for this folder}. Without it:
open Settings, choose the Workspace tab, search for \code{bracketed}, untick
\strong{R: Bracketed Paste}, and close the R terminal.
}

\section{\verb{file is in use} or \verb{cannot remove prior installation} on Windows}{

\strong{Symptom:} Installing or updating a package prints \verb{file is in use},
\verb{cannot remove prior installation of package}, or \code{Warning: restored}.

\strong{Cause:} An open R session has the package loaded, and Windows will not
replace an open file, so R puts the old version back.

\strong{Fix:} With the R Companion, run \strong{AURA: Explain an R error} on the
message and select \strong{Close R terminals and install again}. Without it: quit
VS Code completely, open R on its own, and install again there.
}

